---
/**
 * TabSwitcher — the one switcher control on this site, and the only place its
 * look is decided.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS FOR. A surface that addresses two audiences with the same price
 * and different capabilities, where making a reader scroll past the half that
 * is not theirs is the defect. /pricing is the first, and until 2026-08-03 it
 * had a control that looked like this and switched nothing: the two audience
 * sections were both rendered visible and the pills were plain anchor links.
 *
 * WHAT IT OWNS, AND THEREFORE WHAT A CALL SITE MUST NOT REDECLARE: the trough,
 * the pill geometry, the selected and hover treatment, the focus ring, and the
 * whole of the behaviour. A page supplies the labels and the ids of the sections
 * it already renders. Nothing else.
 *
 * ── THE DESIGN IS THE PRODUCTION CONTROL, MEASURED RATHER THAN EYEBALLED ────
 *
 * Owner, 2026-08-03: "adopt execat same design style what we have in
 * http://localhost:8092/". These figures are read off the running legacy page's
 * `.ptabs` / `.ptab` with getComputedStyle, not copied from its stylesheet,
 * because that page carries several competing rules for those classes and only
 * the browser knows which one won:
 *
 *   trough      display flex · gap 4px · padding 6px · radius 999px
 *               background rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04)
 *   pill        min-height 44px · padding 0 18px · radius 999px · no border
 *               weight 700 · transition 0.2s ease
 *   selected    background #2DD4BF on text #05070E
 *   unselected  transparent on rgba(234, 241, 251, 0.72)
 *
 * Every one of those mapped onto a token this build already had, which was the
 * point: #2DD4BF WAS --c-teal and #05070E IS --c-bg, exactly, so the selected
 * pill was the production pill without a hex being typed.
 *
 * THE SELECTED PILL IS NO LONGER THE PRODUCTION PILL, and that is a decision
 * rather than drift. Owner, 2026-08-04, variant V6: teal means verified and may
 * not appear on a control. A selected tab is the loudest control on the page it
 * sits in and it asserts nothing about evidence, so it takes --c-interactive,
 * which V6 puts at #FFFFFF. The STRUCTURE is unchanged — a filled pill carrying
 * an inverted --c-bg label — and it is the structure that made the reference
 * legible, not the hue. 20.13:1, up from 10.81:1.
 *
 * Three further values are deliberately NOT matched to the pixel, and each is a
 * rule that outranks the reference:
 *
 *   font-size    Legacy pins 14px. Rule 4 of check-design-system.js forbids a
 *                hardcoded font-size in a component stylesheet, and there is no
 *                14px tier: --t-micro clamps to 12-13px, --t-body to 16-17px.
 *                --t-body, which is what the control it replaces already used.
 *   trough tint  --c-card is 0.05 alpha against the legacy 0.04. Using the
 *                site's card tint is worth 0.01 of alpha.
 *   focus ring   Legacy paints 2px teal. This site rings every interactive
 *                element with 3px --c-interactive, and a control that rings
 *                itself differently from its neighbours is the drift the design
 *                system exists to stop.
 *
 * ── WHY THE TROUGH IS A SEGMENTED CONTROL AND NOT TWO OUTLINED PILLS ────────
 *
 * The control this replaces was two separately bordered pills with a gap. Two
 * bordered pills of equal weight say "two links". A single trough holding one
 * filled pill says "one of these two, and it is this one" without needing to be
 * read, which is the affordance the owner found missing. That is the legacy
 * shape and it is kept for that reason rather than out of deference.
 */
interface Tab {
  /** The element id of the section this tab shows. Must already exist on the page. */
  id: string;
  /** The visible label. */
  label: string;
}

interface Props {
  /** Accessible name for the tablist. Say what the choice is, not that it is a choice. */
  label: string;
  tabs: Tab[];
  /** Which tab is selected when the URL names none. Defaults to the first. */
  defaultId?: string;
}

const { label, tabs, defaultId } = Astro.props;

const fallbackId = defaultId ?? tabs[0]?.id;

/*
 * ── THE INLINE BOOTSTRAP, AND WHY IT IS NOT JUST THE MODULE RUNNING EARLIER ──
 *
 * The panels are always rendered visible. That is deliberate and it is argued
 * in scripts/tabs.ts: the legacy page shipped its buyer panel as
 * `style="display:none" hidden` in the HTML, so a reader without working
 * JavaScript lost an entire priced offering with no route to it. The server
 * therefore sends a page that works, and the module upgrades it.
 *
 * The cost of that ordering is a flash. Astro emits the module as a deferred
 * <script type="module">, which runs after the document is parsed, so the
 * browser is free to paint every panel first and collapse them a moment later.
 * On a page the owner is reading for polish, content that appears and then
 * vanishes is worse than the defect being fixed.
 *
 * The module cannot simply run sooner. This control sits in a sticky bar ABOVE
 * the sections it governs, so a synchronous script here cannot reach elements
 * the parser has not got to yet. What it CAN do synchronously is add a rule.
 *
 * The rule is self-cancelling, which is the part worth reading. It hides the
 * un-selected panels only while they are NOT yet tabpanels, and the first thing
 * the module does is set role="tabpanel" on all of them. So the rule governs
 * exactly the window between parse and upgrade, and stops matching the instant
 * real state exists. There is no attribute to clean up, no ordering to get
 * right, and no way for the stylesheet and the module to both think they own
 * visibility.
 *
 * With JavaScript off the script never runs, the rule is never added, and every
 * panel stays visible. The no-JS guarantee is a property of where this lives,
 * not a second code path that has to be remembered.
 *
 * Ids are restricted to a plain identifier shape before being written into a
 * selector. These come from the call site and are section anchors, but a
 * selector built from an unvalidated string is a habit worth not having.
 */
const SAFE_ID = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$/;
const offSelectors = tabs
  .filter((t) => t.id !== fallbackId && SAFE_ID.test(t.id))
  .map((t) => `#${t.id}:not([role="tabpanel"]){display:none}`)
  .join('');
---

<div class="tabsw" data-tabs data-tabs-label={label}>
  {
    tabs.map((t) => (
      <a
        class="tabsw__tab"
        id={`tab-${t.id}`}
        href={`#${t.id}`}
        data-tab={t.id}
        data-tab-default={t.id === fallbackId ? '' : undefined}
      >
        {t.label}
      </a>
    ))
  }
</div>

{/* Emitted unconditionally: with a single tab `offSelectors` is empty and this
    appends an inert stylesheet, which is cheaper to reason about than a
    conditional around a directive. */}
<script is:inline define:vars={{ offSelectors }}>
  /* Wrapped, because define:vars emits a top-level `const` and a page carrying a
     second switcher would otherwise redeclare the same name. */
  (function () {
    var sheet = document.createElement('style');
    sheet.textContent = offSelectors;
    document.head.appendChild(sheet);
  })();
</script>

<script>
  import { initTabs } from '../../scripts/tabs';
  initTabs();
</script>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * The only global rule this component owns. A hidden tabpanel has to be
   * hidden whatever the layout that renders it declares, and the UA rule for
   * [hidden] loses to a single `display` declaration on a class. Two attribute
   * selectors, so it wins without an !important and still cannot reach anything
   * that is not a tabpanel.
   */
  [role='tabpanel'][hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
</style>

<style>
  .tabsw {
    display: inline-flex;
    /* nowrap, matching the reference. Two 44px pills measure ~243px together,
       so the group holds its shape down to 390px without wrapping into
       something that no longer reads as one control. */
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    gap: 4px;
    max-width: 100%;
    padding: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--c-card);
  }

  .tabsw__tab {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* WCAG 2.2 target size, and the reference height. */
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .tabsw__tab:hover {
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /*
   * `aria-selected` IS the selected state. No companion class, so what a screen
   * reader announces and what the pill paints are the same fact, read from the
   * same attribute. See the note in scripts/tabs.ts.
   */
  .tabsw__tab[aria-selected='true'] {
    background: var(--c-interactive);
    color: var(--c-bg);
  }

  .tabsw__tab:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tabsw__tab {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
